{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Let him pursue</b> — Some say: יתרדוף (let him pursue), but there is no need, for there is no dagesh there such that the tav would be swallowed. And moreover יתרדף would have been expected: <b>and trample to the ground my life</b> — on account of the disgrace." "<b>and my glory</b> — its meaning is the soul, as in: \"Let not my soul come into their assembly, and <b>let not my glory be joined</b> unto their company\" (Genesis 49:6), and likewise: \"My heart is glad, and <b>my glory rejoiceth</b>\" (Psalm 16:9), and after it: \"my flesh also dwelleth in safety\" (ibid.) — which is the body. Therefore Rabbi Judah ben Balaam erred, who said that כבודי is my body. Did he not know, if he did not hear, that man is composed of soul and body, and the noble part is the soul? Therefore the word כבוד: and he brought it into this difficulty, because he found <b>and let my glory dwell in the dust, selah</b> — and the soul does not descend to the dust. But the verse is by way of parable; and likewise <b>my soul cleaveth</b> to the dust (Psalm 119:25)." "And the meaning of <b>selah</b> — it was truly so."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:27:13.081161144Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}